[{:seon.task/name "avet-seek-allocates"
  :seon.task/plan "One lookup of an entity by a unique attribute (seon.db/eid: (d/datoms db :avet a v), the same path as datahike.db.utils/entid) costs 13-17 us and 23-32 KB at 100-10k tasks, growing with log n. The work is one seek through 2-3 B-tree nodes with ~6 comparisons each and one datom answered: ~3 us and ~1.3 KB. Find where the seek allocates in our Datahike fork (reference-code/datahike: datahike.db.utils, datahike.index) and our persistent-sorted-set port (reference-code/persistent-sorted-set, E4a) on Jolt, and fix the cause there (profile with seon.profile / seon.cost/measure). Done when seon.task-cost-test/finding-a-task-by-its-id-is-one-index-seek is green with its :overrun key deleted."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-cost-test/finding-a-task-by-its-id-is-one-index-seek}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-small-transaction-costs-its-datoms"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-21: d/with of one 5-datom definition record costs 221-233 us and 148-162 KB, flat in program size (100-10k vars). Estimate from the work: ~15 index inserts at ~1.3 us (seon.cost/units :sorted/assoc) = 20-30 us. ~10x: find Datahike's per-transaction overhead on Jolt (tx report, schema checks, index wrappers) with seon.profile, and fix it in our fork."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-cost-test/a-definition-record-costs-its-datoms}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-schema-transaction-costs-its-attributes"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by session STORES, 2026-10-21 (why a fresh store's open was ~200 ms): the one schema transaction (seon.db/schema, 129 attributes, 407 datoms) costs 45-49 ms even in memory (d/db-with on an empty db; half the schema 15.5-16.7 ms: superlinear), against 0.12 ms for a 2-datom note. Cause: reference-code/datahike src/datahike/db/transaction.cljc:148 update-rschema recomputes the whole rschema (dbu/rschema (:schema db)) after EVERY schema datom (called at :480, :491, :521, :575, :596), so a schema tx of n attributes costs O(n * attributes). Correct: the rschema is derived once per transaction, after its last schema datom (or updated by the one attribute that changed), on a jolt/<topic> branch of our Datahike fork. Estimate: ~407 datom inserts at ~1.3 us plus one rschema derivation over 129 attributes (~50 us): about 1 ms. Since branches-as-in-production the schema tx runs once per store (a check run, the test store), not per boot, so this is no longer on any boot path; it still prices every store's creation and any schema change."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-cost-test/a-schema-transaction-is-linear-in-its-attributes}}
 {:seon.task/name "two-processes-branch-one-store-at-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Since task each-id-is-issued-once (worktree session global-ids, 2026-10-23) entity ids and transaction ids come from one page every process shares (seon.db/ids!), reseeded from the heads at open: of what follows, only the roster append remains. Found by session STORES, 2026-10-21 (asked by session RUNNER: can parallel check steps be several roots on one store?). Writing different branches from several processes is safe and is already the design: each agent process commits its own branch (content-addressed immutable node keys plus its one head key, each put one LMDB write transaction; konserve.jolt.lmdb:86). Changing the roster is not: d/branch! (datahike.versioning/branch!) appends the :branches log with konserve.core/append (reference-code/konserve src/konserve/core.cljc:570), which reads the log head and then makes three separate writes (the element, the old last element's :next, the head) under go-locked, a lock of this process only; konserve.jolt.lmdb's blob lock is a no-op token (lmdb.jolt:28) because LMDB serializes single transactions, not this read-modify-write. So two processes creating branches at once can each link their element after the same last one and one branch drops out of d/branches (its head key stays, so seon.db/branch? still finds it), and a process SIGKILLed between the writes leaves the same tear. The entity-id counter (datahike.writing/eid-allocators) and root's agent leases are per process too. Since task a-lease-is-reserved-from-the-one-counter (worktree session SHRINK2, 2026-10-23) each root reserves its agents' blocks from its own counter (seon.db/lease-block!), which that process seeds once, at its first write, past every branch head then committed: two roots on one store each seed from the same heads, so their own ids and the blocks they hand out start at the same place and overlap (one root cannot see the other's reservations until a head carries them, and a reservation is no head). Their mains never merge into each other, so the overlap stays harmless only while that holds; an agent of one root merging into the other's main, or two roots on one main, would cross entities. The counter is the second reason for one root per store. Production has one root per store, the only process that creates branches (the writer's :spawn and :clone-branch), so it never meets this; several roots on one store at once (parallel check steps as separate roots, a standing bin/root beside a T5 run on <SEON_RUNS>/store) do. Correct: either the roster append is one LMDB write transaction (a konserve.jolt.lmdb -update-in over the three keys in one txn, on a jolt/<topic> branch of our konserve), or the rule 'one root per store at a time' is stated in failure points with its edge (seon.db/open! refusing a store another live root holds, e.g. by an flock on <store>/root.lock). Parallel checks should share one root, each step on its own branch (production's shape), with the crash steps (durable, window, resume, orphan) run with nothing else on the store."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/two-processes-branch-one-store}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-kept-store-gains-new-attributes-at-open"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by root 2026-10-21 restarting the standing root after CONFIG merged: `seon.db/open!` transacted `seon.db/schema` only when it seeded a new store, so a kept store (runs are kept) never gained an attribute added later. Root's boot fact (`seon.task/boot-tx` with `seon.config/facts`) was then refused (`:seon.config.agent/heap-extra-bytes` not in the store's schema), `seon.root/start!` ignored the refusal, and the root ran with no boot fact: `seon.task/root` answered nil, the server's `agents` op failed in `seon.task/entity-value`'s contract, and no task could start. Fix at the owner: `open!` transacts the attributes the store lacks (`seon.db/install-missing!`, one transaction, nothing when nothing is missing; Datahike accepts new attribute definitions at any time), and `start!` ends the boot on a refused boot fact instead of ignoring the answer. Not covered: an attribute whose definition changed (type, cardinality, uniqueness); that is a migration, filed when one is first needed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/opening-a-kept-store-installs-the-attributes-it-lacks}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-store-opens-where-it-lies"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by session NEXTGEN (2026-10-21) reading a kept store for `seon.root/a-root-resumes-a-store-an-earlier-build-wrote`, and on 2026-10-21 copying t5/out/real3.store (this entry absorbs `a-kept-store-opens-from-any-path`, the same cause): `seon.db/store-cfg` named a store by `(hasch/uuid path)` of the path given, and Datahike's connect compares that with the id stored at creation (`datahike.connector/ensure-stored-config-consistency`, :store-identity-mismatch), so a store renamed or copied was refused at its new path: an APFS clone of next/runs/store-full-20261021 (renamed from next/runs/store) answered `Store identity mismatch: connecting to wrong database`. A kept run is a record (owner): it must open wherever it is kept. Fix (worktree session store-native, 2026-10-22): `seon.db/store-cfg` reads the id the store records (`seon.db/stored-id`: the :db head's stored config through Datahike's own read path, `datahike.writing/read-head`, 0.8 ms), and mints `(hasch/uuid path)` only for a store it is about to make. A copy keeps its original's id, as a Datahike replica does (`datahike.store/physical-store-key`), so one process opens one of them. Also fixed in passing: `seon.db/open!` leaked one connection reference when it seeded a store on :db (the seed connection was kept and `open-main!` connected again). Left: `datahike.connector/deref-conn` on a :sync writer rebuilds the db from the stored head with the stored :config, so `(:config @conn)` names the path the store was made at; nothing in src reads the path from there (Seon uses `open!`'s :cfg), and the fix is a line in our Datahike (task `deref-keeps-the-connections-config`). bin/worktree's note (a cloned target/runs/store fails) no longer holds."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-moved-store-opens}}
 {:seon.task/name "deref-keeps-the-connections-config"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by worktree session store-native, 2026-10-22 (task `a-store-opens-where-it-lies`): with a :sync writer, `datahike.connector/deref-conn` answers `(dsi/stored->db stored store)`, whose :config is the one stored at the head, while `datahike.connector/-connect-impl*` builds the connection's db with the caller's config (`(assoc stored-db :config config)`). So after a store is moved, `(:config @conn)` names the path it was made at, and every later commit writes that old :store into the head. Nothing in Seon's src reads the path from `@conn` today, so this is latent. Correct, on a jolt/<topic> branch of our Datahike (root's go first): deref-conn keeps the connection's own config, `(dsi/stored->db (assoc stored :config (:config @wrapped-atom)) store)`, as connect does. Proof: a moved store's `(get-in @conn [:config :store :path])` is its new path."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-moved-store-opens}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-store-grows-with-what-it-keeps"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-22 on the standing root's store (next/runs/store): data.mdb 1,305 MB, up from 747 MB at 2026-10-21 21:56 and 801 MB at 22:15; 278 branches under 68 run mains (every root boot and every check run on this store starts a new main `run-<time>-<id>` beside the old ones; every attempt's branch is kept). Root raised `:seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes` from 1 GiB to 64 GiB on 2026-10-21 after two agents died on Datahike's `{:type :fatal-commit-error}` while recording, without confirming the cause was MDB_MAP_FULL: that hid the growth instead of addressing it (owner: \"What are we covering up and not addressing?\"). Earlier evidence: a kept branch measured 8.0 MB for 1,020 datoms because each per-eval commit rewrites ~18 index nodes (DECISIONS, \"Storage reclaim\"; owner ruling: GC keeps only what live branch heads reach, then compaction, as an :io proc when a measured dead/live ratio passes a config key, or on demand). Correct, in order: (1) measure what fills the store: bytes per branch and per commit, the share unreachable from any branch head, the bytes a run main and its branches hold; (2) state the expected cost of a commit from what it writes (its datoms and the index nodes they touch) and make an overrun a failing cost proof; (3) reclaim: the GC and compaction the ruling names, through Datahike's and konserve's own mechanisms (reference-code/datahike online-gc, konserve), never by deleting a branch; which run mains and branches are kept is the owner's call, asked with the measurement; (4) then set the map from the measured bound, with that provenance. A failing commit's cause is now recorded (task seon.agent/a-death-names-its-cause): confirm whether the two deaths were MDB_MAP_FULL. Measured 2026-10-22 (session LIGHT, test/seon/bench_commit.clj on fresh LMDB stores, bf 64, seeds of 5k/45k/225k datoms): `d/branch!` writes 3 keys, 382 bytes (light); a 1-datom commit 11-14 keys, 16.8-27.7 KB (7-10 index nodes of 1.7-3.6 KB plus the commit and graph records); one eval record (5 datoms) 11-16 keys, 18.6-33.8 KB; 100 evals committed one by one 4.9-6.9 MB with 200-byte printed values and 13.7-19.3 MB with 2 KB ones (49-193 KB per commit), against 157-546 KB for the same 100 in one commit (26-35x). Real turns are worse: `seon.turn/act!` records the context the model read (`:seon.turn/context`), its reply and reasoning, so 50 turns with a 32 KB context committed per turn wrote 62 MB for 1.9 MB of their own values (32x; 8.9 MB at 10 turns per commit, 4.1 MB in one). Root's estimate (each commit rewrites the root-to-leaf paths, ~50 KB, 100 commits ~5 MB) holds for small values; the cause it misses dominates: Datahike holds every value inline in its index leaves (each twice, :eavt and :aevt), appends always go into the tail leaf, and a copy-on-write commit rewrites that whole leaf, so a commit costs about bf/2 x the size of the values already in the tail leaf (leaves of up to 1.3 MB were rewritten per turn), not its own size. Branching factor is not the fix (bf 16: 1.5x less). The run-ahead writer (seon.agent/transact!, commit at idle or after the flush window) already commits about once per turn, the cadence the owner ruled. Fix at the owner: hold values over a threshold out of the index leaves, written once as their own content-addressed key with the datom carrying the reference (Postgres TOAST; v1's seon.blob/inline-or-stage, 4,096 bytes), in our Datahike fork's node codec so every reader sees plain values: emulated, 50 per-turn commits then write ~2.0 MB of index plus each value once (~4 MB at 32 KB contexts, 15x less; 9x at 8 KB). A dependency and format change: root's go first. The proof `seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes` is red: 20 per-turn commits of an 8 KB-context turn wrote 3.84 MB against a bound of 1.07 MB (the one commit, 410 KB, plus 32 KB of path per commit). Owner, 2026-10-22: store each turn's basis, not its context text (session BASIS, jolt/basis): a turn records `:seon.turn/basis` (the commit it read, task, attempt, turn, the proofs' results, red assertions, refusals and run errors, whether unmerged entries show, the last evaluations' transcript while no earlier turn shows them; ~350 bytes) and `:seon.turn/evals` (its own evaluations as the next turns show them), and `seon.context/render` makes the exact text from it on that commit; the turn loop sends that same render. Re-measured with test/seon/bench_commit.clj (seed 2k, bf 64, 50 turns, a 2 KB eval record, 1.5 KB reply, 3 KB reasoning, 2 KB evals): per-turn commits wrote 24.4 MB (488 KB a turn) at an 8 KB context and 61.3 MB (1.23 MB a turn) at 32 KB before, 16.0 MB (321 KB a turn) after at either size (1.5x and 3.8x less); 10 turns a commit 3.5/8.8 MB -> 2.3 MB; one commit 1.6 MB -> 1.0 MB. The proof is still red, 3.84 MB -> 1.63 MB against a bound of 0.84 MB (1.9x over): the remaining gap is the values still held inline in the tail leaves (the reply, reasoning, evals and the eval record's printed value), the out-of-line fix above. A past turn's text needs its commit: a GC that keeps only branch heads must keep the commits turns name, or past contexts become unreadable. Session ONEMAIN, 2026-10-22: checks and tests now delete the branches they made (`seon.db/delete-main!`), but nothing reclaims them: `(datahike.api/gc-storage conn)` on Jolt throws `No dependency provides java.time.Instant — a concrete implementation of the JDK classes must be provided` (after a `:datahike/gc-without-local-writer` warning), so the reclaim needs that class provided first (Jolt's RFC 0014 `:jolt/provides`) or a GC path without it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-entity-id-lookup"
  :seon.task/plan "Session DUP-HELPERS, from notes/duplicates-audit-2026-10-22.md rows A2 and A3. What exists: the entity id of a unique attribute's value, one AVET seek, is written ten times (seon.db/eid public, a private copy in seon.program, inline (:e (first (d/datoms db :avet ...))) in seon.merge three times, seon.source three times, seon.ref and my.schema), and four single-entity Datalog queries do the same job at ~95 us and 100 KB each (seon.config/from-db, seon.error, seon.reconcile pull-end, seon.context). Root's entity ([:seon.root/id :root]) is read six ways, most guarding a pull by lookup ref with an eid check first. The cause: no owner below seon.error and seon.merge, so each namespace wrote its own. The one owner is seon.db (it owns the store and its schema; seon.error, seon.merge, seon.program, seon.source, seon.ref, seon.task and my.schema can all require it, seon.config cannot and needs no eid). seon.db/eid is the one seek; task/eid and program's copy are deleted and every caller says db/eid. A pull by lookup ref already answers nil when the entity does not exist (measured 2026-10-22 on the test store: (d/pull db [:seon.root/boot] [:seon.root/id :root]) is nil before root's first boot), so the eid guards in front of root's pulls go and root's facts are read by the library's own pull (Datahike's lookup ref) everywhere, seon.config/from-db and seon.db/collect! included. Deletes about 15 lines. Wiki: docs/storage.md names seon.db/eid as the one entity lookup."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/eid-is-the-entity-of-a-unique-value seon.task-cost-test/finding-a-task-by-its-id-is-one-index-seek seon.config-test/root-writes-the-config-on-main-and-the-db-answers-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-test-store-leaves-src"
  :seon.task/plan "Session DUP-HELPERS, audit row C7. seon.db/test-store, test-db and test-branch! are used only by test/ (24 test files): test helpers in src, which ships them in every binary and counts them against src's size. They move to test/seon/test_store.clj (seon.test-store, no _test suffix so bin/test runs no tests of it); seon.db/seed! and open-main! become public, as the one way a store is seeded and a main opened. Not moved, against the audit: seon.fixtures/two-calls, a scripted model like its siblings in seon.fixtures, which seon.model resolves by name in the built binary's root (seon.agent-test/in-agent imports only src), where a test namespace is not loaded. No behaviour changes: every test that used them stays green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-test-takes-a-branch-of-the-test-store}}
 {:seon.task/name "dead-readers-are-cleared-at-open"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen by session RUNNER (jolt/runner e0efb69, 2026-10-21): four check steps at once, each its own root and agents on one LMDB store, filled the map: mdb_stat showed 65,534 pages used of 65,536, 60,055 of them free (live data ~85 MB). LMDB reuses a freed page only when no reader's snapshot is older than the txn that freed it, and a SIGKILLed process's reader slot pins its snapshot until mdb_reader_check clears it; nothing on main calls konserve.jolt.lmdb-ffi/reader-check. Correct: clear dead readers where a process opens the env (root boot, agent open: one call), and keep read txns short. The map's size is the config key :seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes; store growth is measured by the store-growth task in this file, which this needs for its measurement."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.db/the-store-grows-with-what-it-keeps"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-dead-readers-slot-does-not-pin-freed-pages}}
 {:seon.task/name "each-commit-names-its-runtime"
  :seon.task/plan "THE PLAN A2 (owner, 2026-10-22: each commit's tx-meta records the runtime fingerprint; DECISIONS, One store holds the whole system). Design and measurements: notes/store-native-2026-10-22.md#q4. The fingerprint is Jolt's own compiled-unit key, `<jolt-version>-<runtime fingerprint>[-tr]` (reference-code/jolt host/chez/loader.ss aot-generation-dir; the runtime fingerprint is baked over a binary's whole image and Chez's boot files, build.ss:2982-3022). From Clojure only `jolt.host/jolt-version` and `jolt.image/runtime-version` are reachable, and neither tells two dirty builds apart, so first (root's go; a jolt/runtime-fingerprint branch): one def-var! `jolt.host/runtime-fingerprint` answering that key. Done (worktree session store-native, 2026-10-22): jolt.host/runtime-fingerprint on jolt/runtime-fingerprint (8cdbb687), `seon.db/tx-meta`, and a string attribute `:seon.tx/runtime` carried in the tx-meta of every transaction Seon makes: the writer's group commit (`seon.writer` commit-batch's arg maps), an agent's `seon.agent/transact!` (`dw/apply!`), and `seon.db/open!`'s seed and install; the fork server already applies main's tx-meta to its replica (`seon.db/report->tx-meta`). One ~90 B datom per transaction, inline. Replaces nothing; adds one attribute."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/the-writers-transactions-name-their-runtime
                      seon.procs-test/a-writers-merge-names-its-runtime
                      seon.agent-test/an-agents-transactions-name-their-runtime
                      seon.agent-test/after-merge-main-the-agent-judges-against-the-new-base
                      seon.db-test/a-kept-store-from-before-the-runtime-attribute-opens}}
 {:seon.task/name "stored-attributes-take-the-new-words"
  :seon.task/plan "Pass 2 of the rename (owner, 2026-10-22, DECISIONS \"Names are Clojure's\"; AGENTS.md section 7a; pass 1 is seon.docs/names-are-clojures-words). The stored attributes: :seon.task/stuck -> :seon.task/blocked; :seon.attempt/waste and :seon.end/waste -> .../overrun (the value schema :seon.learn/waste -> :seon.learn/overrun); :seon.config.contract/guarantees -> :seon.config.contract/instrument; :seon.config.root/entrance-port -> :seon.config.root/server-port (with SEON_ENTRANCE_PORT -> SEON_SERVER_PORT, and SEON_GUARANTEES -> SEON_INSTRUMENT); :seon.ai.model/thinking-dials -> :seon.ai.model/thinking-modes (seon.config/facts writes every config key on main's root entity and every model key on its model entity, so config keys are attributes too; confirm each against (d/schema) of a kept store before migrating it). Not stored, so pure code renames done here with their vars: bin/check's step e2e-stuck -> e2e-blocked; the problem classes :stuck -> :blocked and :waste -> :overrun (seon.problems/report derives them; :seon.problems/class is a schema, not an attribute); seon.reconcile/stuck, stuck-tx, :seon.reconcile/stuck-row -> blocked, blocked-tx, blocked-row; the refusals (Malli's word: the reasons an attempt is not done, as m/explain's errors are): seon.reconcile/done-refusals -> done-explain, :seon.reconcile/refusal -> :seon.reconcile/explain with its :refusal key -> :reason, seon.contract/refusal -> invalid, :seon.print/requery-refusal -> requery-invalid; :seon.attempt/refusal was never an attribute (a test's example key in schema_test and source_test: renamed with the words). Inner keys of :seon.db/data values (:seon.root/quiescent's :stuck) are values: new ones are written with the new key, readers read the new key.
Datahike has no rename of its own (notes: side-session-notes rename-datahike-ident.md, verified at reference-code/datahike): our store is :attribute-refs? false (seon.db/store-cfg sets none; Datahike's default), so datoms hold the attribute keyword; datahike.schema/find-invalid-schema-updates refuses a :db/ident change and datahike.db.transaction/reject-schema-removal-with-current-data refuses retracting an ident with current data; ident and eid map one to one, so there is no alias; datahike.norm runs a tx once and datahike.migrate is export/import. With attribute-refs? true a rename would be native but still refused, and switching modes rewrites every datom.
Plan (forward migration, history kept, nothing rewritten): the schemas declare the new names; seon.db/install-missing! installs them on open (it already adds missing attributes to a kept store); one migration transaction per attribute, a norm keyed by its name and run once (datahike.norm's idea; recorded as a fact so a reopen does nothing): for every entity with a current value under the old name, add it under the new name and retract it under the old. The old attribute stays installed, so its history datoms keep reading (as-of, history) under the old name; current reads see only the new name. Readers of history across the rename ask for both names (none today reads these attributes' history: to confirm by grep of d/history and as-of callers). Measure on a copy of a kept store (bin/worktree root's own runs/store copied under target/, never next/runs/store) at n <= 100 entities per attribute: tx time and datoms written. Root runs it on the standing store at merge, with the owner's go.
Ruling needed (root/owner): the brief asks that old facts read under the new names; that holds for current facts only. Making past datoms read under new names needs ident aliasing in our Datahike (a jolt/ident-alias branch translating names through query, pull, entity and the history indexes in attribute-refs? false mode: large), or the attribute-refs? switch (a full rewrite). Recommended: forward migration as above.
Done: the proofs below green; bin/test --reach green; bin/check smoke, mg-check and instrument OK; one full bin/test and bin/check 100."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.docs/names-are-clojures-words"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-store-made-before-the-rename-reads-its-facts-under-the-new-names
                      seon.db-test/the-rename-migration-runs-once-and-keeps-history}}
 {:seon.task/name "each-id-is-issued-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-23: global addressing everywhere, so data is queryable across branches with d/q, d/pull and d/entity (notes/cross-branch-attack-2026-10-23.md). Entity ids came from per-agent leases of 100,000 (about 5,400 agents per store; a restarted root re-minted a block's unspent tail; two roots on one store overlapped) and transaction ids were per branch ((inc max-tx): two branches forked at one t gave their first transactions one id, measured in notes/global-ids/MEASURED.md §5). Now: one page of two 64-bit words beside the store (native/ids.c, seon.db/ids!), mapped by every process (forked children map it with no step), one atomic fetch-add per id (28-34 ns, 200-240 ns at 10 processes, §6); Datahike takes its ids through datahike.ids (jolt/global-ids: set-ids!, with-store-ids; a transaction takes its id when it begins and :max-tx is its branch's last); the page is moved past every head at open. Deleted: seon.db/lease!, lease-block!, lease-spent, lease-use, :seon.config.store/eid-lease, :seon.db/lease-spent and the :unrecorded path, Datahike's eid ceilings, bin/check lease; the fork server's replica replays main's commit as main's transaction (seon.db/replay). docs/storage.md#ids."
